Osmoregulation is the process by which an organism keeps a stable internal balance of water and dissolved substances whatever the surroundings.
Hydrophytes (aquatic plants) live in marine and freshwater habitats:
A. Marine hydrophytes live in saline (hypertonic) water, where water tends to leave their cells. They:
- excrete excess salts through specialized salt glands;
- synthesize organic solutes (proline, glycine betaine, sugars), which raise their internal osmotic concentration so that they retain water;
- have thick cuticles that reduce water loss, and show halophytic traits to tolerate high salinity.
B. Freshwater hydrophytes live in hypotonic water, where water keeps entering their cells. They:
- expel excess water through hydathodes or vacuoles to avoid overhydration;
- actively absorb ions such as potassium and calcium to keep the osmotic balance in dilute surroundings;
- have thin or absent cuticles and reduced roots, absorbing water and nutrients directly from the water.
Examples: water lily, lotus, seaweeds, tape grass.
Halophytes grow in saline soil or water with high NaCl, MgCl₂ or MgSO₄. Only plants that tolerate a high salt concentration can grow there. Their osmotic adjustments:
- They keep their cell sap more concentrated than the salty soil (by taking up salts and making organic solutes like proline and glycine betaine), so water can still enter the roots (beyond the chapter: the chapter states this for marine hydrophytes);
- succulent (fleshy) leaves and sometimes stems store water and dilute the salts;
- in some, leaves are modified into spines, reducing water loss;
- salt glands excrete excess salt.
Examples: sea arrowgrass, sea lavender; halophytes of marshy seashores form mangrove (tidal woodland) vegetation.
